@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "CI/CD Pipelines";
}
@model DevOpsEduTool.Models.Module

<body>
    <header>
        <nav>
            <div class="logo">
                <a asp-controller="Home" asp-action="Index"
                   style="text-decoration: none; color: inherit">&#8592; Back to the Dashboard</a>
            </div>
            <div class="module-title">Module 3: @Model.Name</div>
        </nav>
    </header>

    <main class="module-container">
        <div id="step-1" class="step">
            <section class="learning-section">
                <span class="step-label">Part 1: Theory</span>
                <h2>The Automation Engine: CI/CD</h2>
                <div class="content">
                    <p>
                        In traditional software development, "Release Day" was a stressful event. Developers had to merge weeks of work manually, often leading to "Merge Hell."
                        <strong>CI/CD</strong> solves this by making integration and deployment a non-event through constant automation.
                    </p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Continuous Integration (CI):</strong> Developers push code to a shared repo several times a day. Each push triggers an automated build and test sequence.</li>
                        <li><strong>Continuous Delivery (CD):</strong> Every "green" build is automatically packaged and ready to be deployed to any environment (Test, Staging) at the click of a button.</li>
                        <li><strong>Continuous Deployment:</strong> The ultimate goal where every change that passes all stages of your production pipeline is released to your customers <strong>automatically</strong>.</li>
                    </ul>



                    <div class="info-box"><strong>Remember:</strong> CI is about code quality and fast feedback; CD is about reliable and frequent releases.</div>
                    <button class="btn" onclick="nextStep(2)">Next: Knowledge Check</button>
                </div>
            </section>
        </div>

        <div id="step-2" class="step" style="display: none;">
            <section class="learning-section">
                <div class="quiz-box">
                    <h4>Knowledge Check: Continuous Integration</h4>
                    <p>A team is struggling with bugs appearing only after merging their code once a week. Which practice would help them most?</p>
                    <form id="cicd-1">
                        <label><input type="radio" name="q1" value="a" /> Implementing CI to merge code daily and run automated tests on every commit.</label><br />
                        <label><input type="radio" name="q1" value="b" /> Sending the code to a separate QA team once a month for manual inspection.</label><br />

                        <button type="button" class="btn" onclick="checkQuiz('cicd-1', 'a')">
                            Check Answer
                        </button>
                    </form>
                    <div id="feedback-quiz-cicd-1" class="quiz-feedback"></div>
                    <div class="navigation-btns">
                        <button class="btn-secondary" onclick="nextStep(1)">Back</button>
                        <button class="btn" onclick="nextStep(3)">Next: The Pipeline Stages</button>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>
        </div>

        <div id="step-3" class="step" style="display: none;">
            <section class="learning-section">
                <h2>Anatomy of a Pipeline</h2>
                <div class="content">
                    <p>
                        A pipeline is a sequence of steps your code must pass. Most modern pipelines are defined as <strong>"Pipeline as Code"</strong> using YAML files.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        <strong>Standard Stages:</strong>
                        <ol>
                            <li><strong>Source Stage:</strong> Triggered when you push code to GitHub/GitLab.</li>
                            <li><strong>Build Stage:</strong> Your code is compiled. In modern DevOps, this usually means creating a <strong>Docker Image</strong>.</li>
                            <li><strong>Test Stage:</strong> Automated unit, integration, and security tests are performed.</li>
                            <li><strong>Deploy Stage:</strong> If tests pass, the container is pushed to a server (like Kubernetes or Azure).</li>
                        </ol>
                    </p>

                    <h4>Example: GitHub Actions Template (US5)</h4>
                    <div class="code-block" style="background: #2d2d2d; color: #ccc; padding: 15px; border-radius: 5px; font-family: monospace;">
                        <pre>
                            jobs:
                              build-and-test:
                                runs-on: ubuntu-latest
                                steps:
                                  - uses: actions/checkout@v2
                                  - name: Run Tests
                                    run: npm test
                                  - name: Build Docker Image
                                    run: docker build -t my-app .
                        </pre>
                    </div>

                    <div class="navigation-btns">
                        <button class="btn-secondary" onclick="nextStep(2)">Back</button>
                        <button class="btn" onclick="nextStep(4)">Next: Knowledge Check</button>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>
        </div>

        <div id="step-4" class="step" style="display: none;">
            <section class="learning-section">
                <div class="quiz-box">
                    <h4>Knowledge Check: Delivery vs Deployment</h4>
                    <p>What distinguishes Continuous <strong>Deployment</strong> from Continuous <strong>Delivery</strong>?</p>
                    <form id="cicd-2">
                        <label><input type="radio" name="q1" value="a" /> Deployment requires a manual approval button before the code goes live.</label><br />
                        <label><input type="radio" name="q1" value="b" /> Deployment releases the code to production automatically as soon as it passes all tests.</label><br />

                        <button type="button" class="btn" onclick="checkQuiz('cicd-2', 'b')">
                            Check Answer
                        </button>
                    </form>
                    <div id="feedback-quiz-cicd-2" class="quiz-feedback"></div>
                    <div class="navigation-btns">
                        <button class="btn-secondary" onclick="nextStep(3)">Back</button>
                        <button class="btn" onclick="nextStep(5)">Next: Practical Simulation</button>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>
        </div>

        <div id="step-5" class="step" style="display:none;">
            <section class="learning-section">
                <span class="step-label">Part 2: Practice</span>
                <h2>Interactive Pipeline Simulation</h2>
                <p>Experience the automation! Click the stages to move your code from a commit to a live running container.</p>

                <div class="pipeline-container" style="background: #1e1e1e; padding: 25px; border-radius: 12px; color: white; border-left: 5px solid #007bff;">
                    <div id="pipeline-log" style="font-family: 'Courier New', Courier, monospace; margin-bottom: 20px; font-size: 0.9em; height: 100px; overflow-y: auto;">
                        <p style="color: #888;">> System ready. Awaiting git push...</p>
                    </div>

                    <div style="display: flex; gap: 10px; flex-wrap: wrap;">
                        <button id="sim-build" class="btn" onclick="runSimulation('sim-build', 'sim-test', 'BUILD: Code compiled into Docker Image.')">1. Run Build</button>
                        <button id="sim-test" class="btn" style="display:none;" onclick="runSimulation('sim-test', 'sim-deploy', 'TEST: Unit & Integration tests passed (100%).')">2. Run Tests</button>
                        <button id="sim-deploy" class="btn" style="display:none;" onclick="runSimulation('sim-deploy', null, 'DEPLOY: Container pushed to Production Cluster.')">3. Deploy to Prod</button>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div id="finish-sim" style="display:none; margin-top: 25px;">
                    <div class="info-box" style="border-color: #42b883; background: rgba(66, 184, 131, 0.1);">
                        <strong>Status: Live!</strong> Your code is now running in production. This automated feedback loop allows teams to innovate faster with less risk.
                    </div>
                    <button class="btn-primary" onclick="completeModule(3, 'module_cicd')">Finish Learning Path</button>
                </div>
            </section>
        </div>

        <footer class="module-footer">
            <script src="~/js/task.js"></script>
            <script src="~/js/progress.js"></script>
        </footer>
    </main>
</body>